1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,880
And I can repeat my same tactics again of saying I'm going

2
00:00:03,880 --> 00:00:05,760
to work hard and then not actually doing it.

3
00:00:06,680 --> 00:00:08,360
Right? So what can employers do

4
00:00:08,560 --> 00:00:10,600
when the threat to fire is no longer scary?

5
00:00:10,600 --> 00:00:14,040
And that is the heart of the efficiency wage model.

6
00:00:14,380 --> 00:00:16,700
So the efficiency wage model says how about this?

7
00:00:17,020 --> 00:00:19,340
How about you first give people a nice raise.

8
00:00:20,480 --> 00:00:22,700
Your workers a nice raise so they're getting paid more

9
00:00:22,700 --> 00:00:25,120
than the competitive amount.

10
00:00:25,640 --> 00:00:29,400
And then you say if I catch you shirking,

11
00:00:29,400 --> 00:00:31,720
catch you slacking, I will fire you.

12
00:00:32,160 --> 00:00:34,880
And now you can't just say who cares, let us move over.

13
00:00:35,340 --> 00:00:37,660
Because now when you move over you're going

14
00:00:37,660 --> 00:00:39,360
to be settling for a lower wage.

15
00:00:39,360 --> 00:00:39,380
Yes?

16
00:00:39,380 --> 00:00:39,380
Yes?

17
00:00:39,380 --> 00:00:44,840
So workers first they will have to find a reputation?

18
00:00:45,220 --> 00:00:48,560
Ah, so that is actually a great question.

19
00:00:48,560 --> 00:00:51,080
So yeah, again in a world where your reputation is known,

20
00:00:51,440 --> 00:00:53,240
that would not be asymmetric information anymore.

21
00:00:54,060 --> 00:00:56,200
Right? So again this is thinking at least

22
00:00:56,200 --> 00:00:59,280
in a lot of labor markets, especially for low-skilled labor

23
00:00:59,280 --> 00:01:01,560
where people really don't know much about you from before.

24
00:01:02,240 --> 00:01:03,480
So I mean they could.

25
00:01:03,480 --> 00:01:07,400
In the United States at least it is normal for employers to lie,

26
00:01:07,940 --> 00:01:10,380
or former employers to lie about you and just say you're great

27
00:01:10,380 --> 00:01:11,000
because they don't want to get,

28
00:01:11,000 --> 00:01:12,260
partly because they don't want to get sued.

29
00:01:12,620 --> 00:01:14,280
And partly because they feel sorry for you.

30
00:01:14,280 --> 00:01:17,120
Yeah, it was great, oh yeah, wonderful.

31
00:01:17,460 --> 00:01:18,620
We were idiots to let them go.

32
00:01:19,300 --> 00:01:20,300
We're fireworks, we actually.

33
00:01:21,580 --> 00:01:21,760
Yes?

34
00:01:21,760 --> 00:01:23,620
Does any other countries have that sort of standard?

35
00:01:24,280 --> 00:01:27,700
So I think it's very likely that they do.

36
00:01:27,700 --> 00:01:29,520
But I'm not sure, but in the U.S. I know.

37
00:01:29,920 --> 00:01:32,480
But yeah, but what I can say is around the world it is normal

38
00:01:32,480 --> 00:01:33,800
to feel bad about firing people.

39
00:01:34,200 --> 00:01:38,640
And normal to go and lie to cover stuff up for someone

40
00:01:38,640 --> 00:01:39,540
that you personally know.

41
00:01:39,820 --> 00:01:42,600
Even if you think the person is actually kind of scum, still,

42
00:01:42,600 --> 00:01:43,960
the scum you know is your scum.

43
00:01:44,600 --> 00:01:47,600
Right? So, yes, you've probably never known,

44
00:01:47,600 --> 00:01:49,700
you've probably got like a bad cousin or something.

45
00:01:49,700 --> 00:01:50,980
And so what is he really like?

46
00:01:51,600 --> 00:01:55,060
Oh, well, he's a good guy I guess.

47
00:01:55,060 --> 00:01:57,880
All right.

48
00:01:58,440 --> 00:02:00,520
So anyway, step one is you raise workers' pay

49
00:02:00,520 --> 00:02:03,480
on the market clearing level in order to make them feel

50
00:02:03,480 --> 00:02:04,700
like they have something to lose.

51
00:02:05,320 --> 00:02:06,880
And then if they do get fired

52
00:02:06,880 --> 00:02:08,860
because you catch them doing something bad, it's going

53
00:02:08,860 --> 00:02:10,720
to be hard for them to get another job that's just as good.

54
00:02:11,940 --> 00:02:13,440
Now the standard question is, yeah,

55
00:02:13,440 --> 00:02:15,440
but if all employers think this way it won't work anymore.

56
00:02:15,900 --> 00:02:17,960
And actually that is not true.

57
00:02:18,960 --> 00:02:21,660
Because if all workers think this way,

58
00:02:21,660 --> 00:02:24,140
so this is regular supply, this is regular demand.

59
00:02:24,140 --> 00:02:27,300
But if all employers go and raise their wage

60
00:02:27,300 --> 00:02:28,700
above the market clearing level,

61
00:02:29,060 --> 00:02:30,540
then what do we get in the market?

62
00:02:32,540 --> 00:02:34,260
Generally, this is, let's let go of it.

63
00:02:34,260 --> 00:02:35,320
Shortage?

64
00:02:35,320 --> 00:02:39,420
Yes. Yes, we get a surplus, which is also known,

65
00:02:39,420 --> 00:02:41,840
a surplus labor, also known as unemployment.

66
00:02:42,340 --> 00:02:45,220
And in a market where there is unemployment, is the threat

67
00:02:45,220 --> 00:02:47,220
to be, is the threat to fire someone scary?

68
00:02:47,220 --> 00:02:48,820
No. Then it becomes scary.

69
00:02:48,820 --> 00:02:49,560
All right.

70
00:02:49,560 --> 00:02:53,640
So either, you know, one person can do it and then it's the fear

71
00:02:53,640 --> 00:02:56,380
of having, getting stuck with a crummy job instead of a good job.

72
00:02:56,720 --> 00:02:58,240
If everyone does it, then it's the fear

73
00:02:58,240 --> 00:03:00,420
of being unemployed rather than having a good job.

74
00:03:00,740 --> 00:03:02,820
So either way, this is a scary threat.

75
00:03:03,340 --> 00:03:05,480
But then there is the side effect of everyone doing it,

76
00:03:05,480 --> 00:03:07,900
which is you have this permanent labor surplus.

77
00:03:09,180 --> 00:03:09,340
All right.

78
00:03:09,820 --> 00:03:12,260
And again, in principle, in the model,

79
00:03:12,420 --> 00:03:14,740
these unemployed workers can be just as productive

80
00:03:14,740 --> 00:03:15,800
as the employed workers.

81
00:03:16,320 --> 00:03:18,460
What's the difference between the employed and the unemployed?

82
00:03:18,960 --> 00:03:19,780
The employed are lucky.

83
00:03:20,700 --> 00:03:22,200
Right? The employed won the lottery

84
00:03:22,200 --> 00:03:23,840
and the unemployed lost the lottery.

85
00:03:24,260 --> 00:03:25,460
The unemployed serve a function,

86
00:03:25,460 --> 00:03:26,860
which is scaring the employed people.

87
00:03:27,800 --> 00:03:30,040
Right? So many people take this model and say, ah,

88
00:03:30,380 --> 00:03:33,100
this is the right way to model Karl Marx's reserve army

89
00:03:33,100 --> 00:03:33,840
of the unemployed.

90
00:03:34,380 --> 00:03:35,620
Ever heard of this one?

91
00:03:35,620 --> 00:03:39,020
There's just a little funny difference between this model

92
00:03:39,020 --> 00:03:40,920
and Marx's reserve army of the unemployed.

93
00:03:40,920 --> 00:03:45,580
In the, in this model, the ideal thing would be if wages,

94
00:03:45,580 --> 00:03:47,120
would be if wages just fell.

95
00:03:47,700 --> 00:03:48,220
All right?

96
00:03:48,480 --> 00:03:50,760
So if wages were just lower, then the problem would be gone.

97
00:03:50,760 --> 00:03:53,520
Where, of course, Marx actually, in this bizarre way,

98
00:03:53,520 --> 00:03:56,420
tries to say, and this is why more wages stay low.

99
00:03:56,900 --> 00:03:57,640
No, no, no, no, no.

100
00:03:57,800 --> 00:03:59,240
This is a model where wages are too high.

101
00:03:59,240 --> 00:04:01,540
It's not a model about why wages are too low.

102
00:04:02,480 --> 00:04:05,400
So, you know, some people have tried to use this

103
00:04:05,400 --> 00:04:07,100
to say this is what Marx was talking about,

104
00:04:07,100 --> 00:04:09,140
but it just doesn't, it doesn't really fit

105
00:04:09,140 --> 00:04:10,040
with what Marx was saying.

106
00:04:10,040 --> 00:04:11,920
And once again, Marx was just wrong.

107
00:04:12,420 --> 00:04:12,600
All right?

108
00:04:12,660 --> 00:04:16,340
So even if there's something that you might take

109
00:04:16,340 --> 00:04:18,540
out of a sentence and say, ah, there's something going on here.

110
00:04:18,820 --> 00:04:18,980
Yes?

111
00:04:18,980 --> 00:04:20,000
Yes?

112
00:04:20,000 --> 00:04:21,620
I'm confused about the demand curve.

113
00:04:21,620 --> 00:04:23,700
Is there like a real demand curve?

114
00:04:23,700 --> 00:04:25,340
Yeah, well, so again, remember, like, you know,

115
00:04:25,340 --> 00:04:27,220
standard model, demand curve is just given

116
00:04:27,220 --> 00:04:28,940
by the marginal revenue product of a worker.

117
00:04:29,560 --> 00:04:31,520
So there are paying workers above the market.

118
00:04:31,640 --> 00:04:32,320
No, so no.

119
00:04:32,320 --> 00:04:35,420
Here, like, here's the key thing is that, so this,

120
00:04:35,420 --> 00:04:36,660
but again, so think about this.

121
00:04:36,660 --> 00:04:37,920
This is the marginal revenue product

122
00:04:38,220 --> 00:04:39,420
with symmetric information.

123
00:04:40,180 --> 00:04:42,340
Right? That's the true marginal revenue product.

124
00:04:42,340 --> 00:04:44,660
That's what you would do if you could be watched

125
00:04:44,660 --> 00:04:45,820
like a hawk all the time.

126
00:04:45,820 --> 00:04:47,180
That's what you're capable of doing.

127
00:04:47,180 --> 00:04:50,980
But then there's the problem that if you just pay people,

128
00:04:50,980 --> 00:04:52,120
if you just pay people that wage,

129
00:04:52,120 --> 00:04:53,660
people feel like they have no particular reason

130
00:04:53,660 --> 00:04:54,480
to actually work that hard.

131
00:04:55,100 --> 00:04:59,680
And so people are not being paid above their actual marginal,

132
00:04:59,680 --> 00:05:02,660
their realized marginal product, but you have to go

133
00:05:02,660 --> 00:05:04,800
and pay them more than that intersection in order

134
00:05:04,800 --> 00:05:06,600
to actually get their full effort out of them.

135
00:05:07,000 --> 00:05:07,580
That's the idea.

136
00:05:08,580 --> 00:05:09,560
Okay. All right.

137
00:05:09,560 --> 00:05:12,140
And if you go and set the model up correctly,

138
00:05:12,260 --> 00:05:16,080
this is a totally stable equilibrium because, you know,

139
00:05:16,080 --> 00:05:18,620
like in the model, so as long as you get the model up right,

140
00:05:18,620 --> 00:05:21,120
if you cut wages a little bit, yes, a bunch of people want

141
00:05:21,120 --> 00:05:22,660
to work for you, but they don't actually work.

142
00:05:23,880 --> 00:05:25,740
Right? So in other words, the productivity

143
00:05:25,740 --> 00:05:29,260
of the workers you get falls faster than the wage.

144
00:05:29,880 --> 00:05:31,200
So if you go and say, hey, I'm going

145
00:05:31,200 --> 00:05:34,160
to pay workers 5% less of that, you get a bunch of workers,

146
00:05:34,160 --> 00:05:37,380
but they're not going to be as productive as they would be

147
00:05:37,380 --> 00:05:38,300
if they were paid a higher wage.

148
00:05:38,300 --> 00:05:40,800
In fact, they'll be, say, 10% less productive than normal.

149
00:05:41,300 --> 00:05:43,880
So if you can get workers that are 10% worse for 5%,

150
00:05:43,880 --> 00:05:46,120
or worse for 5% less, does that sound good?

151
00:05:47,320 --> 00:05:47,680
It's not good.

152
00:05:48,220 --> 00:05:50,480
Right? So that's the idea is

153
00:05:50,480 --> 00:05:52,620
that these other workers who are employed,

154
00:05:52,820 --> 00:05:55,940
even though they're just as good in the model as the ones

155
00:05:55,940 --> 00:05:59,260
that do have jobs, if you went and offered them an amount

156
00:05:59,260 --> 00:06:01,380
of money that made it profitable,

157
00:06:01,380 --> 00:06:04,260
that with an offered amount of money below that amount,

158
00:06:04,780 --> 00:06:07,060
they would actually do a worse job,

159
00:06:07,060 --> 00:06:08,220
and it would not be profitable.

160
00:06:09,480 --> 00:06:11,140
Okay. All right.

161
00:06:11,340 --> 00:06:13,360
Now, the efficiency wage, oh, yeah, yeah, Ash.

