So let's see, so you tax it, so that means the after-tax pay

257
00:10:04,060 --> 00:10:05,760
in the asymmetric sector goes down,

258
00:10:06,180 --> 00:10:11,280
and then actually you subsidize, so that raises post-subsidy wages

259
00:10:11,280 --> 00:10:13,300
in the other sectors, so you're actually spreading the gap

260
00:10:13,300 --> 00:10:14,520
between the two sectors even more.

261
00:10:15,980 --> 00:10:19,500
So people often think about this as like auto workers

262
00:10:19,500 --> 00:10:21,360
versus taxi drivers, and for taxi drivers,

263
00:10:21,360 --> 00:10:23,640
it's symmetric information, and for auto workers,

264
00:10:23,640 --> 00:10:26,700
for some reason, it's asymmetric information, and then the idea is

265
00:10:26,700 --> 00:10:28,940
that we're going and moving workers from these, well,

266
00:10:28,940 --> 00:10:32,560
the low-wage jobs in the taxi sector by taxing those workers

267
00:10:32,900 --> 00:10:36,260
and moving the lucky ones over into the highway sector,

268
00:10:36,800 --> 00:10:39,540
and if this model is right, then it's possible

269
00:10:39,960 --> 00:10:43,000
that total output goes up, and if total output goes up,

270
00:10:43,000 --> 00:10:46,840
then of course, average well-being also goes up,

271
00:10:47,140 --> 00:10:50,040
right, usual model, okay?

272
00:10:50,700 --> 00:10:52,840
Now, there are some other implications

273
00:10:52,840 --> 00:10:53,840
of the efficiency wage model

274
00:10:53,840 --> 00:10:55,480
that have gotten almost no attention

275
00:10:55,480 --> 00:10:57,880
because they horrify the people who like the model,

276
00:10:58,280 --> 00:10:59,260
but they're worth mentioning.

277
00:10:59,760 --> 00:11:03,300
So in the efficiency wage model, you could also solve the problem

278
00:11:03,600 --> 00:11:05,020
by just making unemployment worse,

279
00:11:06,460 --> 00:11:09,260
just make unemployment more scary, right,

280
00:11:09,260 --> 00:11:11,560
and there's one big thing that government does

281
00:11:11,560 --> 00:11:12,840
to make unemployment less scary.

282
00:11:13,440 --> 00:11:14,200
What's that thing again?

283
00:11:15,140 --> 00:11:18,040
Unemployment insurance, so one thing you do is let's get rid,

284
00:11:18,040 --> 00:11:20,860
let's reduce unemployment in the asymmetric information sector

285
00:11:21,180 --> 00:11:22,860
by cutting unemployment benefits,

286
00:11:23,040 --> 00:11:24,240
getting rid of unemployment benefits.

287
00:11:24,240 --> 00:11:26,480
That's totally consonant with the model.

288
00:11:27,020 --> 00:11:28,920
The people who like the model are normally Keynesians

289
00:11:28,920 --> 00:11:29,860
who would be horrified at the thought

290
00:11:29,860 --> 00:11:31,780
of cutting unemployment insurance, but it comes right

291
00:11:31,780 --> 00:11:34,480
out of the model that anything that you're doing

292
00:11:34,480 --> 00:11:35,920
that makes unemployment less scary,

293
00:11:35,920 --> 00:11:38,140
it causes more unemployment, right?

294
00:11:38,720 --> 00:11:42,140
And of course, could be arguing as welfare, and then if you want

295
00:11:42,140 --> 00:11:47,020
to go full Charles Dickens, you could also say, well,

296
00:11:47,020 --> 00:11:49,420
this is an argument for vagrancy laws, saying it's illegal

297
00:11:49,420 --> 00:11:52,280
to not have a job, all right, which,

298
00:11:52,280 --> 00:11:55,680
well such laws have existed 20th century, most prominently

299
00:11:55,680 --> 00:11:57,680
in the Soviet Union where you can be arrested

300
00:11:57,680 --> 00:12:01,940
for not having a job, so, and like especially,

301
00:12:01,940 --> 00:12:04,460
you know, it's a monopsony employer anyway, so you can see

302
00:12:04,460 --> 00:12:06,920
like people wouldn't want those jobs, but yeah,

303
00:12:06,980 --> 00:12:08,880
everyone in the Soviet Union has to have their book explaining

304
00:12:08,880 --> 00:12:10,220
their whole labor history,

305
00:12:10,500 --> 00:12:12,120
solving the asymmetric information problem

306
00:12:12,120 --> 00:12:13,540
to a large degree, right?

307
00:12:13,540 --> 00:12:15,460
If there's a law saying everyone has to have a book

308
00:12:15,460 --> 00:12:18,240
and you can't do anything without someone signing off

309
00:12:18,240 --> 00:12:21,060
and making marks on you, not to advocate it,

310
00:12:21,060 --> 00:12:23,140
just point it out at the model.

311
00:12:23,140 --> 00:12:23,240
Yes.

312
00:12:23,400 --> 00:12:24,960
Uh, so for the subsidy model of work, I guess,

313
00:12:24,960 --> 00:12:27,100
you would have to be tied to keeping the job, right?

314
00:12:27,100 --> 00:12:27,960
You'd have to be tied.

315
00:12:27,960 --> 00:12:29,740
Yeah, yeah, well we give them, well, you subsidize the jobs

316
00:12:29,740 --> 00:12:30,420
in that sector.

317
00:12:30,580 --> 00:12:32,820
Right. So, I mean, you know, like just with like, you know,

318
00:12:32,820 --> 00:12:34,780
so it is a per, it's like a per-unit subsidy

319
00:12:34,780 --> 00:12:37,420
or a per-job subsidy, or maybe a per, maybe an hourly,

320
00:12:37,420 --> 00:12:39,000
hours of work in that industry subsidy.

321
00:12:39,320 --> 00:12:40,580
So yeah, it would be like the investment

322
00:12:40,580 --> 00:12:44,180
of the earned income tax credit, except, you know,

323
00:12:44,180 --> 00:12:45,700
if you thought the whole economy worked this way,

324
00:12:45,700 --> 00:12:46,660
then it would be for any job.

325
00:12:47,020 --> 00:12:49,640
If you believed, as most people who do empirical work,

326
00:12:49,640 --> 00:12:52,400
that the model's relevant in some industries and not others,

327
00:12:53,260 --> 00:12:54,860
then it would only be subsidies

328
00:12:54,860 --> 00:12:57,480
in the special high-value added industries.

329
00:12:58,020 --> 00:12:59,240
And again, there's a common view

330
00:12:59,240 --> 00:13:02,200
that manufacturing has this problem and services don't.

331
00:13:03,060 --> 00:13:05,740
You know, always a little puzzle as to why they think that.

332
00:13:06,340 --> 00:13:09,540
But I mean, I guess it's because wages are higher

333
00:13:09,540 --> 00:13:12,880
in manufacturing, but there is, of course, another explanation

334
00:13:12,880 --> 00:13:14,540
for why wages would be higher in manufacturing.

335
00:13:15,940 --> 00:13:18,840
The civil model, those jobs are more unpleasant, right?

336
00:13:18,840 --> 00:13:23,340
You're in a hot, smelly, smoky, just greasy environment.

337
00:13:24,240 --> 00:13:26,400
So, you know, some people like that, I guess,

338
00:13:26,460 --> 00:13:27,620
but a lot of people do not.

339
00:13:27,900 --> 00:13:29,840
So that would be a simple reason.

340
00:13:30,400 --> 00:13:31,900
Now, just one last point on this.

341
00:13:32,400 --> 00:13:36,960
If you go and read textbooks, including my undergraduate,

342
00:13:36,960 --> 00:13:40,580
my intermediate macro textbook that I had in 1990,

343
00:13:41,000 --> 00:13:43,880
Dornbush and Fisher, when they explain the efficiency

344
00:13:43,880 --> 00:13:45,540
of the wage model, they actually,

345
00:13:45,540 --> 00:13:46,980
the words describe that.

346
00:13:46,980 --> 00:13:49,120
Even though they mean this.

347
00:13:50,120 --> 00:13:53,440
And I remember I took it to Bill Dickens, and I said, hey, look,

348
00:13:53,980 --> 00:13:56,600
doesn't the textbook just go and explain it wrong?

349
00:13:56,600 --> 00:13:58,140
He's like, oh, yeah, I guess it does.

350
00:13:58,880 --> 00:14:03,180
Right? And so when I took it to him, he's saying, well, so,

351
00:14:03,180 --> 00:14:05,560
and this is why mathematical economics adds value,

352
00:14:05,560 --> 00:14:09,460
because if you just use words, then it's easy to go

353
00:14:09,460 --> 00:14:11,600
and confuse these two, whereas if you go and use math,

354
00:14:11,600 --> 00:14:12,820
then you never mix these two up.

355
00:14:13,540 --> 00:14:15,700
And at the time, I was like, maybe, but then afterwards,

356
00:14:15,700 --> 00:14:17,100
I was like, wait a second, Dornbush

357
00:14:17,100 --> 00:14:18,760
and Fisher know the math perfectly well,

358
00:14:19,180 --> 00:14:20,740
but somehow that didn't stop them

359
00:14:20,740 --> 00:14:22,100
from mis-explaining it in words,

360
00:14:23,360 --> 00:14:26,300
which is what I call the problem of poor back translation.

361
00:14:26,600 --> 00:14:28,280
When you go and take words, translate it into math,

362
00:14:28,280 --> 00:14:31,020
and then translate it back into English, a lot of people

363
00:14:31,020 --> 00:14:33,060
who are very good at math are terrible at that last step.

364
00:14:33,060 --> 00:14:36,100
And really, the last step is the one that counts, right?

365
00:14:36,100 --> 00:14:41,120
I think. Not for publication, but for economic understanding.

366
00:14:41,120 --> 00:14:47,600
So, all right, so let us now move on to our next topic here.

367
00:14:47,600 --> 00:14:47,740
Oh, sure.

368
00:14:47,740 --> 00:14:52,220
Is the compensating differential the height difference

369
00:14:52,220 --> 00:14:53,460
between the two supply curves?

370
00:14:53,520 --> 00:14:57,260
So, yeah, the compensating differential is, yes,

371
00:14:57,260 --> 00:14:58,660
the difference in the equilibrium wages.

372
00:14:58,660 --> 00:15:01,140
So, the compensating differential is that amount.

373
00:15:02,280 --> 00:15:04,020
Yes. All right.

374
00:15:05,140 --> 00:15:07,120
Right? And similarly, the efficiency wage would be the

375
00:15:07,120 --> 00:15:10,080
difference between the intersections going in

376
00:15:10,080 --> 00:15:13,060
and the wage that you need in order

377
00:15:13,060 --> 00:15:14,880
to get people to put in effort.

378
00:15:15,640 --> 00:15:17,640
So, again, of course, in the real world, this would be,

379
00:15:17,640 --> 00:15:19,180
again, this would be a continuous variable.

380
00:15:19,460 --> 00:15:23,240
In the famous models, they make the problem easier

381
00:15:23,240 --> 00:15:25,720
by assuming that either you apply effort or you don't.

382
00:15:26,180 --> 00:15:28,680
So, it's a binary decision, and then if it's a binary decision

383
00:15:28,680 --> 00:15:31,480
and all workers are the same, then there's one exact wage

384
00:15:31,480 --> 00:15:32,900
that you need to get up to in order

385
00:15:32,900 --> 00:15:33,860
to get everyone working hard.

386
00:15:34,460 --> 00:15:35,600
Everyone who's employed working hard.

387
00:15:37,600 --> 00:15:38,100
All right.

388
00:15:38,100 --> 00:15:41,220
Yes. Oh, and there's one other solution

389
00:15:41,220 --> 00:15:42,980
to this problem that could also be done.

390
00:15:43,420 --> 00:15:44,620
So, remember we were talking

391
00:15:44,620 --> 00:15:47,240
about contractual punishments earlier on as a solution

392
00:15:47,240 --> 00:15:48,600
to moral hazard problems?

393
00:15:49,720 --> 00:15:50,920
Another application.

394
00:15:51,160 --> 00:15:52,980
Another thing where we're in another case

395
00:15:52,980 --> 00:15:53,800
where we're not using it.

396
00:15:54,300 --> 00:15:58,500
So, the idea here is that if you could go and post a bond saying,

397
00:15:58,500 --> 00:16:00,660
like, if you employ me and you catch me playing Tetris

398
00:16:00,660 --> 00:16:02,800
on the job, I lose my $5,000 bond,

399
00:16:03,400 --> 00:16:04,440
that could solve the problem, too,

400
00:16:04,440 --> 00:16:05,500
and then you could have market clearing.

401
00:16:05,500 --> 00:16:09,500
So, that's another way of doing it.

402
00:16:10,080 --> 00:16:12,720
When you think about it a bit more, there are just built

403
00:16:12,720 --> 00:16:15,200
into the normal job search, something like that,

404
00:16:15,200 --> 00:16:17,700
because in the real world, it's almost never that easy

405
00:16:17,700 --> 00:16:20,420
to find another job, even if you can eventually find another one

406
00:16:20,420 --> 00:16:23,220
just as good, you may be out of work for a week or a month,

407
00:16:23,220 --> 00:16:26,400
and you're stigmatized, and people frown at you and say,

408
00:16:26,400 --> 00:16:27,380
why can't you keep a job?

409
00:16:27,920 --> 00:16:32,180
So, that functions as at least a modest efficiency wage,

410
00:16:32,180 --> 00:16:35,460
because people are, it's like a modest bond,

411
00:16:35,460 --> 00:16:37,880
where if you lose your job, then people get mad at you.

412
00:16:37,880 --> 00:16:41,180
And again, of course, if people are keeping track

413
00:16:41,180 --> 00:16:43,140
of your employment record, that's another way

414
00:16:43,140 --> 00:16:44,360
of solving it by just getting rid

415
00:16:44,360 --> 00:16:45,700
of the asymmetrical information problem.

416
00:16:46,400 --> 00:16:48,060
And you may have noticed that a common piece

417
00:16:48,060 --> 00:16:51,420
of advice is once you get a job, you should not quit that job

418
00:16:51,420 --> 00:16:52,400
for about how long?

419
00:16:53,120 --> 00:16:56,980
Yeah, yeah, people often say at least a year, right?

420
00:16:56,980 --> 00:16:58,100
So, and why?

421
00:16:58,100 --> 00:17:02,260
Because, well, how come you only have that job for a little while?

422
00:17:02,260 --> 00:17:04,800
Yeah, so, my wife once did a job where it was only a month,

423
00:17:04,800 --> 00:17:06,620
but the people hired her had known her for years.

424
00:17:07,060 --> 00:17:10,260
So, then, and the amount, and the raise she got was so large

425
00:17:10,260 --> 00:17:11,580
that almost no one would turn it down.

426
00:17:11,880 --> 00:17:15,020
So, it doesn't reveal that much about your character

427
00:17:15,020 --> 00:17:19,100
if you switch for a raise that almost everyone would go

428
00:17:19,100 --> 00:17:48,100
and switch for.

429
00:17:48,100 --> 00:17:51,980
Oh, yeah, and as I was mentioning, it's very common

430
00:17:51,980 --> 00:17:55,520
for employers to refuse to reveal negative information

431
00:17:55,520 --> 00:17:57,940
about a past employee because they're worried

432
00:17:57,940 --> 00:17:59,480
about legal consequences.

433
00:18:00,200 --> 00:18:03,580
So, if you really were worried about this problem,

434
00:18:03,580 --> 00:18:04,720
how might you change the law?

435
00:18:07,280 --> 00:18:09,300
Well, there's the obvious one, stop punishing people

436
00:18:09,300 --> 00:18:10,760
for saying bad things about workers.

437
00:18:11,180 --> 00:18:13,560
But you could actually switch it around and go further, right?

438
00:18:14,780 --> 00:18:16,340
What, you could be punished for?

439
00:18:16,340 --> 00:18:21,220
Yes, for failing to disclose negative information, right?

440
00:18:21,480 --> 00:18:23,840
Now, technically, that's actually already illegal,

441
00:18:23,840 --> 00:18:25,200
but when I was looking into it,

442
00:18:25,200 --> 00:18:27,160
but that law is pretty much never enforced.

443
00:18:27,740 --> 00:18:28,940
The odds that you'll get sued

444
00:18:28,940 --> 00:18:31,120
because you painted a worker as being better

445
00:18:31,120 --> 00:18:33,760
than they really were, you know, might, you know,

446
00:18:34,480 --> 00:18:38,980
I suppose you might suddenly be attacked by a crazed vigilante

447
00:18:38,980 --> 00:18:41,360
who's angry that you're lying about labor markets or something,

448
00:18:41,400 --> 00:18:43,160
but it's very remote.

449
00:18:43,160 --> 00:18:47,420
All right, so now, finance and portfolio theory,

450
00:18:47,420 --> 00:18:48,860
next set of applications.

451
00:18:50,020 --> 00:18:52,160
All right, so, starting with permanent income anomalies.

452
00:18:52,220 --> 00:18:54,900
So, who here has diminishing marginal utility of consumption?

453
00:18:55,720 --> 00:18:56,820
Diminishing marginal utility of consumption?

454
00:18:56,820 --> 00:18:57,420
All right, good.

455
00:18:57,820 --> 00:19:00,040
And who has access to intertemporal markets?

456
00:19:00,760 --> 00:19:02,080
Yes, all right.

457
00:19:02,760 --> 00:19:05,320
If so, combining these two assumptions,

458
00:19:05,800 --> 00:19:06,800
tailoring your consumption

459
00:19:06,800 --> 00:19:09,720
to your current income makes very little sense, right?

460
00:19:09,720 --> 00:19:10,720
It does not make much sense

461
00:19:10,720 --> 00:19:12,980
to be basing how much you consume now based

462
00:19:12,980 --> 00:19:14,540
on how much you're earning right now.

463
00:19:14,900 --> 00:19:18,440
Instead, the smart thing is to base your consumption upon what

464
00:19:18,440 --> 00:19:25,180
we call your permanent income, or also known

465
00:19:25,180 --> 00:19:27,140
as your expected long-run income.

466
00:19:27,820 --> 00:19:28,840
Your expected long-run income.

467
00:19:29,220 --> 00:19:30,540
This is actually the main insight

468
00:19:30,540 --> 00:19:32,560
that won Friedman his Nobel Prize.

469
00:19:33,560 --> 00:19:36,180
So, if you go and read the citation, it wasn't primarily

470
00:19:36,180 --> 00:19:38,600
for monetarism, more for his theory

471
00:19:38,600 --> 00:19:40,560
of the consumption function published in the 50s.

472
00:19:40,560 --> 00:19:44,020
All right, so now, obviously, there's a lot of truth

473
00:19:44,020 --> 00:19:45,480
in this permanent income hypothesis.

474
00:19:46,020 --> 00:19:47,760
So, take a look at what young people do.

475
00:19:47,760 --> 00:19:49,600
Young people normally pile up a lot of debt,

476
00:19:50,160 --> 00:19:51,760
and then as they get older, they pay it off.

477
00:19:52,420 --> 00:19:54,900
And then, normally, they try

478
00:19:54,900 --> 00:19:57,600
to actually accumulate some assets before they retire.

479
00:19:57,600 --> 00:20:12,580
And then, when they retire, they try to live on that.


